extends ./layout/layout-default
block content
  article.TextPage
    p.Eyebrow BEFORE YOUR FIRST SHIFT
    h1 How to play
    p You decide who gets an interview. Then you train Scout to help and check whether it makes good choices.
    p
      a(href='#objectives') See what you’ll learn
    ol.PlaySteps
      li #[strong Pick who gets an interview.] Open a CV. Choose #[strong Shortlist] to invite them or #[strong Pass] to move on. Aim for 3, then 5, then 8 shortlists. The first round has no timer. The next two give you 45 seconds each. You can keep playing if you miss the target.
      li #[strong Teach Scout.] Send your saved choices to the engineer. Choose a file of old hiring decisions to go with them. Click #[strong Run scanner] to watch Scout make its own choices.
      li #[strong Check a decision.] Open Maya’s message and find the person’s CV. The work test tells you whether they qualified. Compare it with Scout’s answer, then click #[strong Reply to Maya].
      li #[strong Test one change.] Open the case from the city report. Change only city or academy on a copy, then click #[strong Scan both]. Did the decision change, or did it stay the same?
      li #[strong Try a fix.] Compare #[strong Hide city] and #[strong Learn from work tests] on 80 practice CVs. Choose one, then click #[strong See results for all 200 CVs] to compare both versions on new people. The four preview scans are optional.
      li #[strong Look back.] Click #[strong Continue to recap]. Use your own run to answer the recap questions. Then explore the case notes or skip to your receipt, a short report you can download.
    p To take a break, click Pause or press Esc. Click Continue when you reopen the game in the same browser.
    section(aria-labelledby='objectives')
      h2#objectives Learning objectives
      p After playing, you should be able to:
      ul.PlaySteps
        li Tell a #[strong feature] from a #[strong label]. Skill is a detail Scout reads. Your Shortlist or Pass choice is an answer it learns from.
        li Follow a #[strong decision tree]. It works like a flowchart: each answer leads to another question or a final decision.
        li Check for a #[strong shortcut]. Change one detail and see if Scout gives a different answer even though the person’s skill and experience stayed the same.
        li Check whether a fix helps on #[strong new CVs]. Both versions see the same people they didn’t learn from. Count how many qualified people each version missed, and how many it invited who didn’t qualify.
    a.MainButton(href='./game/') Start your shift
